  "problem": "",
  "worked_solution": "Let's start by recalling the graph of $y=\\sqrt{x}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14220666/assets/f8825f71f4d3565b28198b3a4de5424693adfead87a509f38ffecfbccb9fcd01.png)\n\nNow, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false since $f(1)=\\sqrt{1}=1\\ne 2.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, $f(x)=\\sqrt{x}$ is defined for $x\\in [0,\\infty ).$\n\n- Statement III is false since the graph of $f(x)$ lies in the first quadrant.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
